WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.360
- I really needed to turn
over the reigns to Bob,

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.596
to someone else.

00:00:04.596 --> 00:00:06.060
- [Narrator] That was Robert Iger in 2020,

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.980
when he stepped down as CEO of Disney,

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.170
the company he led for 15 years.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.760
Two years later, he's now back,

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.590
after the board ousted Bob Chapek

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.810
following weak fourth quarter earnings.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.660
Disney stock rallied
after the announcement.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.780
Bringing back a CEO is a
strategy that many companies

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.250
have attempted over the years,

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.260
some more successfully than others.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.702
This is how three so-called
Boomerang CEOs made comebacks,

00:00:28.702 --> 00:00:32.250
in how their company's
fared after their return.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.730
- A former CEO can help
revive a company's culture.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.460
They're often viewed as
the face of the company.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.583
They often have inspired
the hearts and minds,

00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:44.010
a lot of people sometimes say.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:47.010
Bob Iger was someone that
while he was not a founder,

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.290
people have described
him as a quasi founder,

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.440
in the sense that he was
a larger-than-life CEO.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.273
- [Narrator] Like Iger,

00:00:53.273 --> 00:00:56.387
Howard Schultz was considered
a popular CEO at Starbucks.

00:00:56.387 --> 00:00:59.430
He first stepped down as CEO in 2000,

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:03.330
but returned in 2008 as the
coffee chain's sales slumped.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.840
After cost cuts, Schultz got
Starbucks to profitability.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.342
By the time he stepped down again in 2017,

00:01:09.342 --> 00:01:13.650
Starbucks' stock was up 640%.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.520
But when he returned once more
in 2022, on an interim basis,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.587
he found a different company,
facing worker dissatisfaction

00:01:20.587 --> 00:01:23.100
and pandemic consumer shifts.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.370
- I came back to reinvent
the role and responsibility

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:27.990
of a public company at a time

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.800
where there is a cultural
and political change.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:32.820
- [Narrator] Starbucks has said

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.880
that it will invest $1
billion into employees

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.241
and store experience.

00:01:37.241 --> 00:01:39.563
- A returning CEO or founder

00:01:39.563 --> 00:01:42.510
has to come back to a different company.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.490
So remember, someone
else has been leading it

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.785
for usually a couple of years, at least.

00:01:46.785 --> 00:01:49.800
That means the strategy has often shifted.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.886
So Spencer Stewart
looked at across all CEOs

00:01:52.886 --> 00:01:57.330
that were appointed at S and
P 500 companies since 2010.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.870
And eight out of 13 permanent
boomerang appointments

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.725
did worse during their second stint

00:02:02.725 --> 00:02:04.680
than during their first stint.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.600
- [Narrator] Twitter's
co-founder, Jack Dorsey,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.825
was fired in 2008, in part
for frequent absences.

00:02:09.825 --> 00:02:12.735
- The board brought him back in 2015.

00:02:12.735 --> 00:02:14.610
They basically wanted

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.190
to reassure investors, and the hope was

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.970
to revive the social media
company's sagging user growth.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.110
- [Narrator] During his second run,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.498
some employees said he
acted too slowly on issues

00:02:24.498 --> 00:02:27.540
like hate speech,
harassment, and censoring.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.660
Dorsey left the CEO post again in 2021.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.297
During his second tenure,
Twitter shares gained 63%,

00:02:33.297 --> 00:02:36.720
while the NASDAQ composite
more than doubled.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.940
Twitter and Dorsey did
not immediately respond

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.320
to request for comment.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.120
When he left, he said in a statement

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.570
that founders leading companies
can be severely limiting.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.720
But founders like Dorsey
are common boomerang CEOs.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.555
- What we often see is that
CEOs who are either founders

00:02:51.555 --> 00:02:56.555
or really connected to companies
feel that it's their duty,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:01.140
their service to make sure
that the company succeeds.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.993
One of the most prominent
examples from the late 90s

00:03:02.993 --> 00:03:06.240
is Steve Jobs coming back to run Apple

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.060
when it was on the brink of bankruptcy.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.550
- [Narrator] Jobs first
left Apple in 1985.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.190
He returned in 1997 as an advisor,

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.650
and eventually took the CEO title.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.300
- I, like a lot of other people,

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:21.376
are pulling together to help
Apple get healthy again,

00:03:21.376 --> 00:03:24.660
and I am extraordinarily confident

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.220
that that is gonna happen.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:27.594
- [Narrator] He quickly
brokered a surprise deal

00:03:27.594 --> 00:03:30.200
with Microsoft, sending the stock up,

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.660
and he brought Apple back to
profitability within a year.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.970
By the time he stepped down in 2011,

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.330
Jobs had helped turn Apple
into a smartphone giant.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:42.420
Apple did not immediately
respond to a request for comment.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.190
As for Iger, he now has two
more years at the top at Disney.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:47.820
Investors hope he can turn things around,

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.253
despite the company's tumultuous year.

